@H2 2.2 Documents Referenced but Not in the Record
@P The following instruments are referred to in the record but were not produced. Each is material, and each should be obtained before the draft is circulated to Pryor Whitfield & Koss or to Bayside's counsel.
@C1 (a) The **Environmental Land Use Restriction** recorded March 22, 2018 at Volume 412, Page 88 — the operative restrictions (including the definition of "residential use" and the activity and use limitations) must be read in full. The Phase II summary paraphrases it, and the paraphrase itself flags undefined terms.
@C1 (b) The **Declaration of Right of First Refusal** dated September 8, 2019, recorded Volume 405, Page 55 — the Title Commitment describes it but does not reproduce it; the amendment we require cannot be drafted without it.
@C1 (c) The **Declaration of Restrictive Covenants** dated November 15, 2019, recorded Volume 408, Page 230 (affordable housing).
@C1 (d) The **Final Judgment (Order) of Condemnation** by which the Authority acquired the fee in 2012.
@C1 (e) The **Phase I ESA** dated March 15, 2025 (HEC-2025-0318) and the full Phase II report with Appendices A–J.
@C1 (f) The **survey plat** (Sheets 1 and 2).
@C1 (g) The **Coventry Capital Partners LP preferred equity documents** (11% preferred return; 70/30 promote above a 15% IRR hurdle) — these are not incorporated into the ground lease but they drive consent, transfer, and pledge requirements.
@C1 (h) The **construction contract, GMP, budget, and schedule**.
@C1 (i) The **revenue pro forma** supporting the $28,500,000 percentage-rent breakpoint. There is no revenue projection anywhere in the record; we therefore cannot test whether the breakpoint is set at, below, or above stabilized revenue.
@C1 (j) The **project program** — unit count, gross floor area, parking count, and the affordable unit schedule. The Term Sheet describes the Residential Tower as containing "a number of residential dwelling units to be determined and specified in the Definitive Lease." The 15% affordable obligation, the percentage-rent analysis, and the occupancy tests all depend on a number that does not exist in the record.
