@H2 8.2 Negotiation posture on the five critical items
@P For your call with Howard Koss, our recommended sequence is: (1) the reverter, because it is the item that determines whether the deal finances, and because the Authority's own board resolution frames it as an enforcement mechanism rather than a windfall, which gives us room to offer a remedy that actually enforces; (2) the tax abatement commencement date, because it is a pure economic item with an arithmetic answer and does not require the Authority to abandon a principle; (3) percentage-rent indexation, where we can offer to keep the 2.0% rate and the $28.5M starting point and move only the trajectory; (4) the legal description and mechanics' lien, which are not negotiable at all and should be presented as corrections rather than as requests; and (5) the ROFR amendment, which we should route through Bayside's counsel so that it is understood as a lender requirement rather than a tenant concession.
@H2 8.3 Escape hatches
@P We recommend that you preserve the following, and we have drafted them into the Lease: a termination right if the conditions precedent are not satisfied by December 31, 2025 (Section 2.7); day-for-day extension of the Milestone Dates and the Rent Commencement Date for delay in satisfying any condition precedent, so that a slow Authority does not put you behind the reverter clock (Sections 2.7 and 9.3(c)); an offset remedy for a failure to deliver the abatement (Section 6.2(d)); and a consent-deemed-granted mechanism with a fifteen-business-day deadline and a self-help right (Sections 10.9 and 16.7).

